SingleTon Class

A Singleton class is a class that allows only one object (instance) of itself to be created during the entire runtime of the application.

👉 In other words:

You cannot create multiple instances using new.

You get the same object every time you access it.

It’s used when you need one shared resource throughout the application (e.g., logging, configuration, DB connection pool manager).


package com;

public class Employee {
    private static Employee emp; // Step 1: a static reference to the single instance

    private Employee() {         // Step 2: private constructor (cannot use new outside this class)

    }

    public static Employee getEmployee() {   // Step 3: static method to provide the object
        if(emp == null)                      // if no object is created yet
            emp = new Employee();            // create the first object
        return emp;                          // return the same object for all future calls
    }
}


How it works:

private static Employee emp;
→ Stores the single object of this class. static means it belongs to the class, not objects.

private Employee()
→ Constructor is private. So, no one can call new Employee() outside this class.

public static Employee getEmployee()
→ This is the global access point.
→ On the first call, it creates the object (emp = new Employee()).
→ On later calls, it returns the same object.


Key Points:::

Ensures only one instance of the class exists.

Provides a global point of access (getEmployee()).

Used in: Database connections, Logger, Caches, Thread pools, Config managers.

